  "step_2": "The individual considered the similarity in the distribution of probabilities between A and B, noting that both had probabilities around 1/3. They focused on the difference in probability: B has a 34% chance (1% higher than A's 33%) of winning. The individual mentions that the monetary difference (2500 vs. 2400) was small, so they prioritized the slightly higher probability of winning in B. Their justification centers on the 1% higher chance of winning in B and the minimal difference in potential gains, leading them to choose B based on this probabilistic advantage.",
  "step_3": "The decision reason requires preferring the more right-skewed lottery. Lottery A is more right-skewed (67% vs. 66% on unfavorable outcomes), so the reason would favor A. However, the individual chose B, which is less right-skewed. The individual did not mention skewness or the distribution of probabilities in terms of favorability/unfavorability. Instead, they focused on the higher probability of winning (34% vs. 33%) and the small monetary difference. Their justification is based on maximizing the probability of a non-zero outcome, not on the skewness of the distribution. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based reason to make the decision. The information considered (probabilities of winning and payoff difference) does not align with the skewness criterion, and the justification does not reference skewness or the relative mass of unfavorable outcomes. The decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

