  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates the skewness of the lotteries. Skewness refers to the asymmetry in the distribution of outcomes: a lottery is right-skewed if a larger portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable (lower) outcomes compared to favorable (higher) ones. In this case, Lottery A has a single outcome (500 Euros with 100% probability), so it is not skewed (it is a degenerate distribution). Lottery B has 50% probability of 1000 Euros and 50% probability of 0 Euros. Since half the probability is assigned to the unfavorable outcome (0 Euros), and half to the favorable (1000 Euros), it is symmetric in terms of probability mass, not right-skewed. However, because the outcome space is asymmetric (0 vs. 1000), and the lower outcome (0) has the same probability as the higher one, it is actually left-skewed (more mass on the low side relative to the high side). But crucially, neither lottery is right-skewed: Lottery A has no skew, and Lottery B is left-skewed. Therefore, the reason cannot prefer a lottery that is more right-skewed, because neither lottery is right-skewed. Thus, the reason cannot apply meaningfully to this decision problem as defined, since there is no lottery that is right-skewed, and the preference rule cannot be triggered. The reason is not applicable.",
